WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.118
Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold
water, while calcium reacts more readily.

00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:08.648
Learn specific comparisons rather than
ranking every metal from one slogan.

00:00:09.432 --> 00:00:14.321
One outer electron, easy loss,
stronger reactions down group one.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.301
That is the chain I need

00:00:16.646 --> 00:00:19.316
Now the halogens, our school group seven.

00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:24.922
Fluorine, chlorine, bromine, and iodine
are non-metals with seven outer electrons.

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.655
Their familiar elemental forms
contain pairs of atoms, such

00:00:28.656 --> 00:00:30.760
as chlorine molecules Cl2.

00:00:31.528 --> 00:00:33.255
Seven outer electrons.

00:00:33.604 --> 00:00:37.244
Instead of losing one, gaining
one can complete the outer shell.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.935
That is the useful pattern
for forming halide ions.

00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:46.714
At room temperature, fluorine is a pale
yellow gas, chlorine a yellow-green gas,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:52.077
bromine a red-brown liquid, and iodine a
dark solid that can give a purple vapor.

00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:55.876
Gas, gas, liquid, solid.

00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:59.695
Their melting and boiling points
increase down that sequence.

00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:03.499
Does chemical reactivity
increase down this family too?

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:09.428
Bigger atoms again, so I am tempted to say
yes, but that sounds like your trap voice.

00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:11.576
It is the opposite trend.

00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:16.293
Down the familiar halogens, increased
distance and shielding weaken

00:01:16.293 --> 00:01:18.191
attraction for an incoming electron.

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:22.324
Gaining one becomes less
favorable, so reactivity decreases.

00:01:23.032 --> 00:01:26.238
Same ideas about distance
and shielding, different job.

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:29.648
Group one loses an
electron, halogens gain one.

00:01:30.428 --> 00:01:31.223
Exactly.

00:01:31.541 --> 00:01:35.973
A more reactive halogen can displace a
less reactive one from a halide solution.

00:01:36.541 --> 00:01:39.573
Chlorine can displace
bromine from sodium bromide.

00:01:39.943 --> 00:01:43.693
Bromine does not displace chlorine
from sodium chloride under the

00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:45.350
usual classroom conditions.

00:01:46.158 --> 00:01:50.261
Displacement is a prediction from
relative reactivity, not just which

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.704
bottle has the brighter color.

00:01:52.424 --> 00:01:52.824
Good.

00:01:53.226 --> 00:01:55.488
Halogens and their vapors can be harmful.

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:59.567
We use labeled animations and
teacher-managed demonstrations,

00:01:59.904 --> 00:02:02.866
never smelling or opening
sample containers ourselves.

00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:05.190
On the far right are the noble gases.

00:02:05.376 --> 00:02:08.337
Helium, neon, and argon are our examples.

00:02:08.755 --> 00:02:12.275
Their outer shells are full in
this simple model, making them very

00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:14.407
unreactive under ordinary conditions.

00:02:15.252 --> 00:02:17.122
Helium has two, not eight.

00:02:17.329 --> 00:02:20.854
It has a full first shell, so
it still belongs in the family.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:22.126
Correct.

00:02:22.313 --> 00:02:26.101
We say very unreactive, not
incapable of any reaction ever.

00:02:26.527 --> 00:02:30.764
Helium is useful in balloons because it
is less dense than air and non-flammable.

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:34.631
Argon provides a protective atmosphere
around hot metal in welding.

00:02:35.418 --> 00:02:38.581
And neon can glow orange-red
in a discharge lamp.

00:02:39.084 --> 00:02:41.817
Different jobs, same useful chemical calm.

00:02:42.607 --> 00:02:48.291
In 1869, Dmitri Mendeleev organized
known elements largely using atomic

00:02:48.292 --> 00:02:49.945
weights and chemical properties.

00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:54.812
He left gaps and predicted properties
of undiscovered elements rather than

00:02:54.814 --> 00:02:57.243
forcing every card into the wrong family.

00:02:58.026 --> 00:03:02.152
A gap was a testable prediction,
not unfinished homework.

00:03:02.906 --> 00:03:03.686
Exactly.

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:06.898
Discoveries such as germanium
supported those predictions.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.728
The modern proton number ordering
later resolved difficulties with

00:03:10.743 --> 00:03:12.555
ordering only by atomic weight.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:15.954
Scientific models improve
when new evidence arrives.

00:03:16.751 --> 00:03:20.766
Final challenge: two atoms
have six protons each but

00:03:20.766 --> 00:03:22.082
different neutron numbers.

00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:23.163
Different elements?

00:03:23.941 --> 00:03:24.228
No.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.371
Isotopes of the same element.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.731
Proton number fixes the identity.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.413
Different neutron numbers
change mass number.

00:03:32.147 --> 00:03:35.692
Why are lithium and sodium
chemically similar, and why is

00:03:35.695 --> 00:03:37.879
potassium generally more reactive?

00:03:38.653 --> 00:03:41.031
One outer electron
gives similar chemistry.

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:44.273
Farther down the group,
distance and shielding make

00:03:44.276 --> 00:03:45.974
that electron easier to lose.

00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:49.204
I must explain the loss,
not just quote the arrow.

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:50.651
What happens

00:03:50.651 --> 00:03:54.218
to halogen reactivity down the
familiar sequence, and why?

00:03:54.974 --> 00:03:55.897
It decreases.

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:00.862
Greater distance and shielding weaken
attraction for an incoming electron.

00:04:01.218 --> 00:04:04.305
They are gaining, not
losing, in that comparison.

00:04:05.197 --> 00:04:05.889
Excellent.

00:04:06.207 --> 00:04:10.567
Groups down, periods across, proton
number for identity, and outer

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:12.268
electrons for chemical patterns.

00:04:12.573 --> 00:04:15.818
Our seating plan has become a
scientific explanation, and my

00:04:15.818 --> 00:04:17.893
imaginary marking pen is smiling
